[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  thursday august 11th i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  on wednesday samsung revealed the latest  versions of its foldable smartphones the  galaxy z fold 4 which becomes the size  of a small tablet when opened and the  more compact galaxy z flip for  for customers who may be growing weary  of how connected they are to their  devices the company has a pretty simple  pitch here's kim swanson's from  samsung's marketing and sales team at  wednesday's launch event describing the  perks of foldable phones  they change smartphone behavior shifting  how we use our devices and giving us  more control over what we're doing in  the moment when you open your phone you  can really immerse yourself in focus and  the simple act of closing your device  signals to your friends that they have  your undivided attention  but they also have drawbacks compared to  other smartphones their screens are more  delicate and their price tags are hefty  still samsung isn't the only company  betting that foldables are the way of  the future  joining me to discuss why is our  personal tech reporter dalvin brown  hi dalvin thanks for joining me thanks  for having me  so let's start with kind of why samsung  does think more consumers are going to  want to buy their their foldable phones  what changes has it made to to make  these phones these new generation of  their foldables more appealing  yeah so four years after  launching its first foldable samsung's  new devices look a lot like the old ones  from the outside but what's been changed  is the ruggedness so samsung  really focused a lot of attention on the  crease that's created when opening and  closing its foldable phones there's also  a panel that protects the screen and  samsung attached it with  with a better adhesive so that it  wouldn't peel off as it has in previous  models so really samsung focused  primarily on the durability of these new  devices  okay so this is the fourth generation of  the phone they're improving durability  but how have sales been so far and i  guess how is samsung expecting them to  be  yeah so samsung says that foldables are  here to stay and is doubling down on  them  consumers have been buying these phones  more and more each year  last year samsung's foldables were the  most popular  foldable devices in the globe but  samsung is also the largest smartphone  maker but it's also important to put  those numbers into context so foldable  phones represent less than one percent  of overall smartphones  in the market today and so while  they are becoming more popular among a  subset of users they're still just a  drop in the bucket in terms of like  overall smartphone sales  what about the competition for making it  i mean you mentioned that samsung was  the the biggest seller of foldables but  is it the only one  samsung is not the only one there are  some other notable names xiaomi oppo and  lenovo's motorola  also have foldable phones  and  apple holds patents for foldable phones  so there may be one in the future  so right now samsung's fold and the flip  they both have pretty steep price tags  is competition going to affect that  yeah so just like with  ordinary smartphones that we're all  familiar with foldables are expected to  come down in price over time  samsung initially launched its fold  at two thousand dollars and that price  has dropped about two hundred dollars  since then its galaxy z flip has dropped  by three hundred eighty dollars since  its first launch so  we're expecting prices to continue to  fall some analysts told me that they  expect that foldables will always be  premium so we may never see a 400  foldable just because the parts and  components are so are so expensive but  also as the price comes down more  consumers may be interested in  purchasing one  why bother with a foldable i mean i can  understand that for a user there's this  kind of nice convenience of having a  slightly smaller phone when you want it  to be small and bigger when you want it  to be bigger  but from  the makers standpoint this seems  complicated it's expensive you've got to  get people to shell out a lot more money  to buy them  why bother going this route  well if you look at it most smartphones  look the same today they are black glass  boxes with a couple cameras on the back  and year after year that's what  consumers get so  what smartphone makers are looking  to do  is break away from that and  differentiate themselves from the  competition and so what foldable phones  do or more flexible screens do is they  allow companies a chance to  create something new that consumers  haven't seen  and to put their own spin on it okay but  when do these foldables become fixtures  of the market i mean when is this  actually going to change kind of the the  broader smartphone market and not just  be something that samsung is offering  yeah so analysts that i spoke to said  that foldables may never overtake the  bar shaped smartphones we're  all accustomed to today  but they do speak to something larger  which is just like creating more nimble  screens or maybe even rollable  screens that pull out from a device and  allows you to sort of double the space  of your phone without having to add a  hinge to it  so they may never be as popular as the  devices that we're familiar with today  but they are certainly picking up in  popularity and a subset of consumers are  noticing  all right that's our personal tech  reporter dalvin brown thanks for joining  us dalvin thanks for having me as always  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts i'm zoe thomas for the  wall street journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  you

